      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 466,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two atoms of **Hydrogen**\n*   One atom of **Oxygen**\n\nThese atoms are bonded together tightly, creating a molecule that has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This \"polarity\" is what makes water such a great solvent—meaning it can dissolve many substances, like salt and sugar.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, which fills our oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils or evaporates.\n\nPure water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless. \n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\nWater is often called the **\"cradle of life.\"** \n*   **For the Planet:** Water covers about 71% of the Earth's surface, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It drives our weather, shapes the landscape, and regulates the global climate.\n*   **For Living Things:** Water is the primary component of life. The human body, for example, is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a medium for metabolic reactions, transports nutrients, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Without water, carbon-based life as we know it cannot survive.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the lifeblood of our planet."
